section: compute
title: Compute and pools
about: |
  The machine pools of the cluster: their size, their instance types, and what each engine needs
  of them before Palette can build the pool.
rules:
- id: instance-offering
  title: The instance type is offered in each zone of the pool
  engines:
  - aws
  - eks
  - gcp
  - gke
  tier: palette
  severity: fail
  status: source
  when: .entity.spec.cloudAccountUid != null
  what: |
    An instance type that a zone does not offer fails during the build: `Unsupported`,
    `ZONE_RESOURCE_POOL_EXHAUSTED`, or a node group `CREATE_FAILED`. The instance type route of
    Palette lists each type of the region. The field `type` of an entry names the type, and
    `nonSupportedZones` names the zones that do not offer it, and is `null` when each zone offers
    the type. One request answers every pool. The check fails for a pool whose type lists a zone
    of the pool in `nonSupportedZones`, or whose type the region does not list. A pool with no
    zone is checked against the list of the region alone. The list is the catalog of Palette, not
    the cloud, so the reproduce line asks the cloud.
  check:
    kind: palette
    method: GET
    path: /v1/clouds/{{ .accountKind }}/regions/{{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }}/instancetypes?cloudAccountUid={{ .entity.spec.cloudAccountUid }}
    expr: |
      def zones: ((.cloudConfig.azs // []) + [ (.cloudConfig.subnets // [])[] | .az // empty ]) | unique;
      .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region as $region
      | if (.response.ok | not) then ["Palette cannot list the instance types of region \($region): \(.response.body.code // .response.status): \(.response.body.message // "")"]
        elif ((.response.body.instanceTypes | type) != "array") then ["Palette gave no list of instance types for region \($region)"]
        else .response.body.instanceTypes as $types
          | [ .entity.spec.machinepoolconfig[]
              | .poolConfig.name as $pool | (.cloudConfig.instanceType // "") as $t | select($t != "")
              | ([ $types[] | select(.type == $t) ] | .[0]) as $have
              | if $have == null then "pool \($pool): the type \($t) is not in the list of region \($region)"
                else (($have.nonSupportedZones // []) as $no | zones | map(select(. as $z | ($no | index($z)) != null)) | .[] | "pool \($pool): the type \($t) is not offered in the zone \(.)")
                end ]
        end
  reproduce:
    aws:
    - 'aws ec2 describe-instance-type-offerings --location-type availability-zone --filters Name=instance-type,Values={{ .entity.spec.machinepoolconfig[0].cloudConfig.instanceType }} --region {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }} --query ''InstanceTypeOfferings[].Location'' --output text   # each zone of the pools must be in the list'
    eks:
    - 'aws ec2 describe-instance-type-offerings --location-type availability-zone --filters Name=instance-type,Values={{ .entity.spec.machinepoolconfig[0].cloudConfig.instanceType }} --region {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }} --query ''InstanceTypeOfferings[].Location'' --output text   # each zone of the pools must be in the list'
    gcp:
    - gcloud compute machine-types describe {{ .entity.spec.machinepoolconfig[0].cloudConfig.instanceType }} --zone {{ .entity.spec.machinepoolconfig[0].cloudConfig.azs[0] // "<zone>" }} --project {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project }} --format 'value(name,zone)'
    gke:
    - 'gcloud compute machine-types list --filter ''name={{ .entity.spec.machinepoolconfig[0].cloudConfig.instanceType }}'' --project {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project }} --format ''value(zone)'' | grep {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }}   # each zone of the region that offers the type'
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Refuse a pool whose zone is in nonSupportedZones of its type in the validate route.
  evidence:
  - kind: source
    note: the instance type route lists nonSupportedZones, the zones that do not offer the type, for each type of a region
  - kind: run
    ref: '#404'
    note: the route of AWS names the type of an entry in `type`, and an entry has no `name`
  - kind: run
    ref: '#411'
    note: the route of AWS gives `null` in `nonSupportedZones` for most types, and an array for the others
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/aws/instance-zone-not-offered.json
    clean: fixtures/aws/instance-zone-offered.json
  - fault: fixtures/aws/instance-type-not-listed.json
  - fault: fixtures/gcp/instance-zone-not-offered.json
    clean: fixtures/gcp/instance-zone-offered.json
  - clean: fixtures/aws/instance-zone-null.json
  - clean: fixtures/gcp/instance-zone-null.json
- id: spot-capacity
  title: Spot capacity is short
  engines:
  - aws
  - eks
  tier: race
  severity: warn
  status: source
  what: |
    A pool of capacity type spot can find no capacity at the moment of the build. No check before
    the build answers it; the watch sees the capacity events.
  evidence:
  - kind: doc
    ref: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/spot-interruptions.html
- id: eks-worker-needed
  title: An EKS cluster needs one worker pool that is not Fargate
  engines:
  - eks
  tier: static
  severity: fail
  status: source
  what: |
    The Palette agent cannot run on the EKS control plane. With no worker node, the cluster is
    never `Running`. One node group with a size of 1 or more is needed.
  check:
    kind: static
    expr: |
      if ([ .entity.spec.machinepoolconfig[]
            | select((.poolConfig.isControlPlane // false) | not)
            | select((.poolConfig.size // 0) >= 1) ] | length) == 0
      then ["no worker pool with a size of 1 or more; the Palette agent needs a node group"]
      else [] end
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Refuse an EKS create with no worker pool in the validate route.
  evidence:
  - kind: doc
    ref: https://docs.spectrocloud.com/clusters/public-cloud/aws/eks/
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/eks/no-worker.json
    clean: fixtures/eks/clean.json
